      <h1>Best Pubs to Watch Darts in London</h1>
      <p class="lead-text">When December arrives and the PDC World Darts Championship takes over Alexandra Palace, London transforms into the undisputed capital of world darts. Over 90,000 spectators make the pilgrimage up the hill to Ally Pally’s Great Hall, but thousands more descend upon London’s pubs and sports bars to soak up the drama, sing the terrace chants, and watch the tungsten fly on supersized screens.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="container content-layout">
    <main class="main-column">
      <VerificationBadge verifiedDate="October 2026" topic="London Venue Information" />

      <section class="content-card">

<p>Finding the right venue is not just about having a screen on the wall. Darts demands an authentic pub environment: pristine television sightlines where you can actually see the double-top wire, dedicated commentary audio turned up for big sessions, cold pints served without a 30-minute queue, and an electric crowd that erupts in unison at every 180 and 170 checkout.</p>
<p>Whether you are looking for a lively pre-match pint within walking distance of the palace gates, an all-day viewing headquarters in Central London, or a traditional boozer where you can throw a few legs yourself between sessions, this curated guide breaks down London's premier darts venues by zone.</p>
<p>Planning your journey to North London? Check our comprehensive <a href="/travel/">London Travel &amp; Accommodation Hub</a> and study the fastest transit connections on our <a href="/travel/tube-train-routes/">Ally Pally Tube &amp; Train Route Guide</a>.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="content-card">
<h2>How We Picked the Best Darts Pubs</h2>
<p>London boasts thousands of licensed premises, but only a select few deliver the sensory experience darts fans expect during the festive major season. Every pub included in this guide was evaluated against four strict editorial criteria:</p>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Screen Visibility and Sightlines:</strong> We prioritized venues featuring large high-definition screens and well-positioned wall displays to ensure clear viewing angles of the dartboard from key seating and standing areas.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Sports Atmosphere:</strong> Darts demands an energetic environment where fans can follow the drama. We selected established sports bars and popular match-day hubs known for showing major televised events, with several venues prioritizing live commentary audio for high-profile sessions.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Capacity, Flow, and Queues:</strong> Festive sessions draw huge crowds. We selected venues with efficient multi-till bar layouts, dedicated cellar cooling to keep pints crisp under heavy demand, and enough floor space to prevent gridlock.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Distance and Accessibility to Alexandra Palace:</strong> For match-day fans, proximity to the hill is vital. We mapped exact walking times to the Great Hall entrance, verified bus connections, and identified the nearest London Underground and National Rail stations so you are never stranded when late legs finish.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>For guidance on what to expect inside the arena itself, review our <a href="/venue/">Alexandra Palace Venue Guide</a> and check session start times in our <a href="/tickets/">World Championship Ticket &amp; Session Guide</a>.</p>
      </section>
      <AdSlot position="in-article" />
      <section class="content-card">
<h2>Near Alexandra Palace &amp; Wood Green (Match-Day Picks)</h2>
<p>If you hold tickets for an afternoon or evening session—or if you simply want to soak up the infectious match-day energy alongside costumed fans before walking up the hill—these North London venues are your prime staging grounds.</p>
<p><code>[ Alexandra Palace / The Phoenix ]
                                   ▲
        (10–12 min steep walk)    │     (12–15 min park walk)
                                  │
    [ The Victoria Stakes ] ──────┴────── [ The Starting Gate ]
        (Muswell Hill N10)                    (Station Road N22)
                                                      │
                                             (Opposite Rail Station /
                                             12-min walk Wood Green Tube)</code></p>
<h3>The Starting Gate (Wood Green / Alexandra Palace Station)</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Address:</strong> Buckingham Road / Station Road, Wood Green, London, <strong>N22 7SS</strong></li>
<li><strong>Nearest Station:</strong> <strong>Alexandra Palace Rail Station</strong> (directly opposite) or <strong>Wood Green Tube</strong> (Piccadilly Line, 12-minute walk)</li>
<li><strong>Distance to Ally Pally:</strong> 12–15 minute walk uphill through Alexandra Park</li>
<li><strong>Atmosphere &amp; Screens:</strong> Positioned directly opposite Alexandra Palace rail station, The Starting Gate is one of the primary meeting points for attendees arriving by train before walking up the hill. A traditional Victorian pub featuring an outdoor patio garden and bar screens showing live sports, it regularly draws event crowds and darts fans during the World Championship. Because screen priority and audio settings depend on the pub's daily sports schedule, checking with staff on arrival is recommended if you plan to watch specific matches.</li>
<li><strong>Top Tip:</strong> Due to its location right by the station, it becomes busy ahead of session start times. Allow extra time if stopping for a pre-event drink before making the uphill walk into the park.</li>
</ul>
<h3>The Victoria Stakes (Muswell Hill)</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Address:</strong> 1 Muswell Hill, London, <strong>N10 3TH</strong></li>
<li><strong>Nearest Station:</strong> <strong>Highgate Tube</strong> (Northern Line, then short W7 bus) or <strong>Finsbury Park</strong> (W7 bus to bottom of hill)</li>
<li><strong>Distance to Ally Pally:</strong> 10–12 minute walk uphill to the Palm Court entrance</li>
<li><strong>Atmosphere &amp; Screens:</strong> Located at the foot of Muswell Hill near the entrance to Alexandra Park, The Victoria Stakes is an established neighborhood pub and dining room. It features a covered courtyard and interior screens showing live televised sport, serving as a popular food and drink stop for visitors to the area. With a strong focus on quality dining, table bookings are recommended if you plan to sit down for a meal before walking up to the palace.</li>
</ul>
<h3>The Mossy Well (Muswell Hill Broadway)</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Address:</strong> 258 Muswell Hill Broadway, London, <strong>N10 3SH</strong></li>
<li><strong>Nearest Station:</strong> <strong>Highgate Tube</strong> (Northern Line) or <strong>Finsbury Park</strong> (via W7 bus)</li>
<li><strong>Distance to Ally Pally:</strong> 15–20 minute walk across the park ridge</li>
<li><strong>Atmosphere &amp; Screens:</strong> Housed in a former express dairy building on Muswell Hill Broadway, this expansive Wetherspoon pub features high ceilings, a large beer garden, and multiple television screens showing Sky Sports. With substantial floor space and standard pub fare, it offers a reliable, budget-friendly option for larger groups visiting the area.</li>
</ul>
<h3>The Phoenix Bar &amp; Kitchen (Inside Alexandra Palace)</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Address:</strong> Alexandra Palace Way, London, <strong>N22 7AY</strong></li>
<li><strong>Nearest Station:</strong> <strong>Alexandra Palace Rail</strong> or <strong>Wood Green Tube</strong> (W3 bus stops outside)</li>
<li><strong>Distance to Ally Pally:</strong> 0 minutes (located directly in the Palm Court complex)</li>
<li><strong>Atmosphere &amp; Screens:</strong> Located inside the palace complex itself, The Phoenix offers panoramic views across London from its terrace and dining area, along with bar screens showing broadcast coverage during event days. Note that access during major championship sessions is often subject to event admission rules or restricted to ticket holders, so check entry conditions on arrival.</li>
</ul>
      </section>
      <section class="content-card">
<h2>Central London (Big-Screen Sports Pubs)</h2>
<p>Do not have tickets for the Great Hall? You can still capture the cauldron-like energy of the championship by joining hundreds of passionate fans at Central London’s dedicated sports bars.</p>
